  {
   "type": "flow",
   "head": "Speed, and what std::sort does",
   "items": [
    {
     "label": "Average",
     "text": "O(n log n)",
     "sub": "pivots split evenly",
     "cue": "fast on average"
    },
    {
     "label": "Worst case",
     "text": "O(n²)",
     "sub": "bad pivot every time",
     "cue": "degrades"
    },
    {
     "label": "The fix",
     "text": "introsort",
     "sub": "what std::sort uses",
     "cue": "introsort"
    },
    {
     "label": "Inside it",
     "text": "quick + heap + insertion",
     "sub": "not stable",
     "cue": "switches to heap sort"
    }
   ],
   "say": "Quicksort is fast on average, O of n log n, when pivots split evenly. Always picking the last item of an already sorted array degrades it to O of n squared. Real libraries fix this. S t d colon colon sort is an introsort: quicksort with a good pivot, that switches to heap sort if recursion gets too deep, and uses insertion sort on tiny ranges. So it is O of n log n in the worst case, but not stable."
  },
